course: money
lesson: 34
title: Inflation explained with a chocolate bar
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Inflation means prices in general going up over time, so the same pound buys a little less each year.
explain: Picture Nan saying, "When I was your age, a chocolate bar was 5p!" and Kai holding one that costs 80p: that is 80p ÷ 5p = 16 times more, but wages went up over those years too, so people can still buy chocolate.
explain: When the news says inflation is 3%, it means things cost about 3p more for every pound than a year ago, on average; some prices jump more, some less, and a few, like TVs, even fall.
explain: If your pocket money stays £5 while sweets go from 50p to 55p, you get 9 sweets instead of 10; grown-ups feel the same when pay goes up £2 for every £100 but prices go up £5 for every £100.
explain: Experts argue about what causes prices to jump fast; to slow them down the central bank can make borrowing dearer, which helps savers but hurts families paying back a mortgage.
fact: Inflation is prices going up over time; the inflation rate is how much they rise in a year, as a percentage.
fact: A chocolate bar going from 5p to 80p costs 80p ÷ 5p = 16 times more; wages also rose over those years.
fact: A bar going from 50p to 55p rose by 5p, and 5p is one tenth of 50p, which is a big jump for one year.
fact: Inflation of 3% means about 3p more for every pound on average: a £1 thing becomes £1.03 and a £10 thing £10.30.
fact: Purchasing power is what your money can buy: £5 buys 10 sweets at 50p but only 9 at 55p, with 5p left.
fact: A knock-on effect is when one change causes others: if lorry fuel goes up 20p a litre, a 100-litre trip costs 100 × 20p = £20 more, so food in the shops costs more.
fact: It was reported that prices rose very fast in many countries in 2022; economists disagree about the causes, and several things probably happened at once.
fact: Some people blame energy prices, some blame too much money being created, and some blame big companies raising prices; each view has supporters.
fact: Central banks raise interest rates to cool spending and slow inflation, but loans cost more: a mortgage can go from £600 to £700 a month, £100 more.
fact: Hyperinflation is extremely fast price rises: if prices doubled every month, a £1 loaf would be £2, £4, £8 and then £16 after 4 months.
fact: It was reported that hyperinflation happened in Germany about 100 years ago and in Zimbabwe more recently, making money nearly useless.
fact: Some things get cheaper as they get easier to make: a TV that was £600 might be £400 now; when prices fall overall, it is called deflation.
fact: Real wages are pay compared with prices: if £100 of pay becomes £102 but £100 of shopping becomes £105, a worker is £3 short.
exercise: What is inflation? | Prices in general going up over time | Pocket money going up every year | Balloons being blown up at a party | Banks paying more interest on savings
exercise: A bar cost 5p when Nan was young and costs 80p now. How many times more? | 16 times | 75 times | 8 times | 85 times
exercise: Ella's bar went from 50p to 55p. How much did it go up? | 5p | 55p | 10p | 105p
exercise: 5p more on a 50p bar is what fraction of the old price? | A tenth | A fifth | A half | A quarter
exercise: Inflation is 3%. What will a £10 thing cost next year, on average? | £10.30 | £13 | £10.03 | £3
exercise: The news says inflation is 3%. What does Dad say it means? | Things cost about 3p more for every pound | Everything in every shop costs exactly £3 more than before | Only three things went up in price | Prices fall by 3p for every pound
exercise: Mia's £5 buys sweets at 55p each. How many can she buy? | 9 | 10 | 11 | 5
exercise: How much change is left after buying those 9 sweets with £5? | 5p | 55p | 50p | 45p
exercise: Lorry fuel goes up 20p a litre and a trip uses 100 litres. How much more does the trip cost? | £20 | £2 | £120 | £200
exercise: Why does dearer petrol make bread and milk dearer too? | Lorries carrying food cost more to run | Bread and milk are partly made out of petrol | Shops have to add petrol to the food by law | Farmers drink petrol to keep warm in winter
exercise: On the news, people argue about what causes high inflation. What is true? | Experts disagree, and several causes may happen at once | Everyone agrees energy is the only cause | Everyone agrees it is caused by children | Inflation has no causes at all
exercise: Why does the central bank raise interest rates? | To cool spending and slow inflation | To make all prices double overnight | To give every family a free mortgage | To stop people saving any money
exercise: Mum's mortgage goes from £600 to £700 a month. How much more is that? | £100 | £1,300 | £70 | £10
exercise: If prices doubled every month, what would a £1 loaf cost after 4 months? | £16 | £8 | £4 | £32
exercise: What is hyperinflation? | Extremely fast price rises that make money nearly useless | Prices staying exactly the same for years | Prices falling a little each year | A big sale in every shop at once
exercise: A TV was £600 and a better one is now £400. How much cheaper? | £200 | £1,000 | £400 | £100
exercise: Sam's dad's pay goes up £2 for every £100, but prices go up £5 for every £100. On £100, how much short is he? | £3 | £7 | £2 | £5
test: Nan says, "A Mars bar was 5p in my day!" Your cousin goes, "No way, so Nan was minted." ("Minted" means rich.) What's the truth? | Prices were lower but wages were lower too | Nan was rich, because everything was cheap | Nan had more pocket money than any child now | People were paid in chocolate then
test: Ella yells, "Who put my chocolate up?!" Her brother says, "The shop's just greedy, end of." What's the fuller answer? | Many things push prices up, like ingredients, energy and transport | Shops pick prices by spinning a wheel each morning | Only chocolate ever goes up in price; everything else always stays the same | Prices never go up, so the shop made a mistake
test: On the news, one person says "energy prices", another says "too much money printed", another says "big companies putting up prices". Which is the fair summary? | Experts disagree, and each view has supporters | The first person is right and the others are lying | The last person is right and nobody disagrees | None of them is talking about inflation at all
test: Mum, sarcastic: "Brilliant, cheers," when rates go up and her mortgage rises from £600 to £700. Grandad, with savings, is pleased. Why? | Higher rates make loans dearer but savings earn more | Higher rates help everyone equally, whether they borrow or save | Higher rates make Grandad's savings smaller and smaller | Mum is truly happy and Grandad is only being sarcastic
test: What would happen if prices doubled every month for 5 months, starting with a £1 loaf? | The loaf would cost £32 | The loaf would cost £10 | The loaf would cost £16 | The loaf would cost £5
test: A Gen Alpha kid says, "My fiver's lowkey mid now, it buys less." ("Mid" means not great.) Sweets went from 50p to 55p. How many fewer does £5 buy? | 1 fewer: 9 instead of 10 | 5 fewer: 5 instead of 10 | None fewer: still 10 | 2 fewer: 8 instead of 10
test: Ruby goes, "TVs are cheaper than before, so inflation's fake!" What's the best reply? | Some things fall while most rise; inflation is the average | She's right, inflation was made up by the news | TVs are the only thing that ever changes price | TVs get dearer every year, so she's wrong
test: In a cartoon, the king fixes high prices by printing a mountain of money for everyone. What would really happen? | With the same goods and more money, prices would shoot up | Everyone would be rich for ever with no problems | Prices would fall because money is everywhere | The shops would close because money is boring
test: Sam's dad says, "2% rise, prices up 5% — so I'm worse off, mate." His mate, a cheery Aussie, says, "Yeah nah, you got a raise, you're sweet." ("Yeah nah" means no; "sweet" means fine.) Who's right? | Sam's dad: if prices rise faster than pay, he can buy less | His mate: any raise at all means you're better off than before | Both: pay and prices don't affect each other in any way at all | Neither: prices and pay always rise at exactly the same speed
test: Inflation is 3%. Mum's weekly shop is £100 — no, £200. Roughly what will the same shop cost next year? | £206 | £203 | £230 | £300
test: Grandad says, "In some countries folk needed wheelbarrows of money for a loaf." What is this called, and where was it reported? | Hyperinflation, reported in Germany long ago and Zimbabwe more recently | Deflation, reported only in Britain last year | Low inflation, reported in every country | Interest, reported only in banks
test: A lorry uses 100 litres and fuel rises 20p a litre. The firm makes 10 such trips a week. How much more a week? | £200 | £20 | £2,000 | £120
exam: It is given that an article formerly priced at fifty pence is now priced at fifty-five pence. Determine what fraction of the former price the increase represents. | One tenth | One fifth | One half | One fifty-fifth
exam: Ascertain the price, after a period of four months, of an article initially priced at £1, its price doubling at the conclusion of each month. | £16 | £8 | £4 | £5
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises inflation? | A general rise in prices over time, whereby a given sum purchases fewer goods | A general fall in prices over time, whereby a given sum purchases more goods | An increase in the quantity of air contained in an article | A fixed charge imposed by the government upon chocolate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the causes of elevated inflation. | Economists disagree, and several causes may operate concurrently | Economists unanimously attribute it to a single cause | It arises exclusively from the conduct of children who spend their pocket money | It has no cause and cannot be influenced by any measure
